Calls.Text:
React re-renders are based on **state updates and component execution**, not simply whether prop values “look” unchanged.

Two common reasons apply here:

### 1. Parent re-renders cause children to render by default

When a parent’s state updates, React executes the parent again. Its child components also render unless React can skip them, typically with `React.memo`.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const config = { color: "blue" };

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

Every time `Parent` renders, `{ color: "blue" }` creates a **new object reference**. Although its contents are identical, JavaScript considers it different:

```js
{ color: "blue" } === { color: "blue" } // false
```

Therefore, even a memoized child sees `config` as changed.

### 2. Stabilize the object and memoize the child

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div style={{ color: config.color }}>Hello</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const config = useMemo(() => ({ color: "blue" }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>
        Count: {count}
      </button>
      <Child config={config} />
    </>
  );
}
```

`useMemo` preserves the object reference until one of its dependencies changes, while `React.memo` lets React skip the child when its props are shallowly equal.

If the object depends on another value:

```jsx
const config = useMemo(
  () => ({ color: selectedColor }),
  [selectedColor]
);
```

Other options include:

- Define a constant object outside the component if it never changes:

  ```jsx
  const CONFIG = { color: "blue" };
  ```

- Pass primitive values instead of an object when practical:

  ```jsx
  <Child color="blue" />
  ```

Also note: if the state being updated belongs to `Child` itself, then `Child` **must re-render** regardless of unchanged props. `React.memo` only helps with re-renders caused by the parent; it does not block the component’s own state or consumed context updates.

In short: an inline object has a new identity on every render. Use `React.memo` together with a stable prop reference when avoiding the child render provides a meaningful performance benefit.

