Calls.Text:
React re-renders a component whenever:

1. Its own state changes.
2. Its parent re-renders—unless the component is memoized.
3. A context it consumes changes.

Also, objects are compared by **reference**, not by contents. If the parent creates the object inline, each render produces a new reference:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Update</button>
      <Child options={{ color: "blue" }} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Even though `{ color: "blue" }` has the same values, it is a new object on every parent render.

### Fix with `React.memo` and `useMemo`

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div style={{ color: options.color }}>Child</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const options = useMemo(() => ({ color: "blue" }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Update</button>
      <Child options={options} />
    </>
  );
}
```

`React.memo` lets `Child` skip rendering when its props are shallowly equal, while `useMemo` keeps the object reference stable.

An even simpler option is to pass primitive values:

```jsx
<Child color="blue" />
```

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ color }) {
  return <div style={{ color }}>Child</div>;
});
```

Note that if the state update occurs **inside `Child`**, it will still re-render regardless of unchanged props—state updates are supposed to trigger rendering. Also, in development, React Strict Mode may intentionally render components more than once to detect unsafe side effects.

